narrative:
She's leaning against a waymarker like she's been waiting exactly as long as necessary and not a moment longer - the same trick she pulled the first time you met her. This time, though, she keeps glancing past your shoulder at the road behind you, and hasn't stopped since you came into view.

@Saoirse: Good. You're still walking upright and asking questions instead of getting yourself killed by not asking them. That's more than I expected, honestly.

She doesn't relax when she says it. If anything, she gets more watchful.

You help her scrub the trail clean the old way - false trails, a name quietly retired, a route north that doesn't connect to anything Marrow's people would think to check. By dawn, Saoirse Bellwether has, functionally, stopped existing, and someone else entirely has taken the next stretch of road in her place.

@Not-Saoirse: Thank you. For all of it. If we meet again, I likely won't be using this name - so if a stranger ever seems to know you better than they should, you'll understand why.

She's gone before full light, and the road feels a little emptier for it.

@Saoirse: All right. All right - you're right, and I hate that you're right. Running bought me three years. It was never going to buy me peace.

She doesn't leave immediately - there's a route to plan, evidence to gather, allies to consider carefully before walking back into danger with open eyes instead of a hidden name. But something in how she carries herself has already changed, the specific weight of a decision finally made rather than endlessly deferred.

@Saoirse: This isn't goodbye yet. But when it is, I want you to know it was you who tipped it. For whatever that's worth to carry.

@Saoirse: Stay, then. Gods help you, but stay.

It's not a clean resolution - Marrow's reach doesn't simply vanish because you asked nicely - but it's a real one, the two of you facing whatever comes next together instead of Saoirse facing it, as she has for three years, entirely alone.

She falls into step beside you like she's done it a hundred times before, which, in every way that matters except the literal one, she basically has.

He talks more than he probably should, the particular looseness of someone who's just had a very bad night and hasn't recalibrated yet. Not everything - he's still loyal enough, or frightened enough of his own employer, to hold some of it back. But enough.

@Watcher: She's not just cleaning up old loose ends. There's a bigger move coming, timed to the succession - she wants every witness to anything inconvenient handled before anyone starts asking harder questions than usual. You're not the only name on a list like mine.

Saoirse's expression doesn't change. Whatever she's feeling, she's keeping it entirely off her face.
